[L24] Active Record
[L25] 
[L26] Active Records are special forms of DTOs. They are data structures with
[L27] public (or bean-accessed) variables; but they typically have navigational
[L28] methods like save and find. Typically these Active Records are direct
[L29] translations from database tables, or other data sources.
[L30] 
[L31] Unfortunately we often find that developers try to treat these data structures
[L32] as though they were objects by putting business rule methods in them. This
[L33] is awkward because it creates a hybrid between a data structure and an
[L34] object.
[L35] 
[L36] The solution, of course, is to treat the Active Record as a data structure and
[L37] to create separate objects that contain the business rules and that hide their
[L38] internal data (which are probably just instances of the Active Record).
[L39] 
[L40] 
[L41] 
[L42] Conclusion
[L43] 
[L44] Objects expose behavior and hide data. This makes it easy to add new kinds
[L45] of objects without changing existing behaviors. It also makes it hard to add
[L46] new behaviors to existing objects. Data structures expose data and have no
[L47] significant behavior. This makes it easy to add new behaviors to existing
[L48] data structures but makes it hard to add new data structures to existing
[L49] functions.
[L50] 
[L51] In any given system we will sometimes want the flexibility to add new data
[L52] types, and so we prefer objects for that part of the system. Other times we
[L53] will want the flexibility to add new behaviors, and so in that part of the
[L54] system we prefer data types and procedures. Good software developers
[L55] understand these issues without prejudice and choose the approach that is
[L56] best for the job at hand.
[L57] 
