[L1] At the same time, many developers fear that a large number of small,
[L2] single-purpose classes makes it more difficult to understand the bigger
[L3] picture. They are concerned that they must navigate from class to class in
[L4] order to figure out how a larger piece of work gets accomplished.
[L5] 
[L6] However, a system with many small classes has no more moving parts than
[L7] a system with a few large classes. There is just as much to learn in the
[L8] system with a few large classes. So the question is: Do you want your tools
[L9] organized into toolboxes with many small drawers each containing well-
[L10] defined and well-labeled components? Or do you want a few drawers that
[L11] you just toss everything into?
[L12] 
[L13] Every sizable system will contain a large amount of logic and complexity.
[L14] The primary goal in managing such complexity is to organize it so that a
[L15] developer knows where to look to find things and need only understand the
[L16] directly affected complexity at any given time. In contrast, a system with
[L17] larger, multipurpose classes always hampers us by insisting we wade
[L18] through lots of things we don’t need to know right now.
[L19] 
[L20] To restate the former points for emphasis: We want our systems to be
[L21] composed of many small classes, not a few large ones. Each small class
[L22] encapsulates a single responsibility, has a single reason to change, and
[L23] collaborates with a few others to achieve the desired system behaviors.
[L24] 
[L25] Cohesion
[L26] 
[L27] 
[L28] 
[L29] Classes should have a small number of instance variables. Each of the
[L30] methods of a class should manipulate one or more of those variables. In
[L31] general the more variables a method manipulates the more cohesive that
[L32] method is to its class. A class in which each variable is used by each
[L33] method is maximally cohesive.
[L34] 
[L35] In general it is neither advisable nor possible to create such maximally
[L36] cohesive classes; on the other hand, we would like cohesion to be high.
[L37] When cohesion is high, it means that the methods and variables of the class
[L38] are co-dependent and hang together as a logical whole.
[L39] 
[L40] Consider the implementation of a Stack in Listing 10-4. This is a very
[L41] cohesive class. Of the three methods only size() fails to use both the
[L42] variables.
[L43] 
[L44] Listing 10-4 Stack.java A cohesive class.
[L45]    public class Stack {
[L46]      private int topOfStack = 0;
[L47]      List<Integer> elements = new LinkedList<Integer>();
[L48] 
[L49]      public int size() {
[L50]        return topOfStack;
[L51]      }
[L52] 
[L53]      public void push(int element) {
[L54]        topOfStack++;
[L55]        elements.add(element);
[L56]      }
[L57] 
[L58]      public int pop() throws PoppedWhenEmpty {
[L59]        if (topOfStack == 0)
[L60]          throw new PoppedWhenEmpty();
[L61]        int element = elements.get(--topOfStack);
[L62]        elements.remove(topOfStack);
[L63]        return element;
[L64]     }
[L65]    }
[L66] 
[L67] The strategy of keeping functions small and keeping parameter lists short
[L68] can sometimes lead to a proliferation of instance variables that are used by a
[L69] subset of methods. When this happens, it almost always means that there is
[L70] at least one other class trying to get out of the larger class. You should try to
[L71] separate the variables and methods into two or more classes such that the
[L72] new classes are more cohesive.
[L73] 
[L74] Maintaining Cohesion Results in Many Small Classes
[L75] 
[L76] 
[L77] 
[L78] Just the act of breaking large functions into smaller functions causes a
[L79] proliferation of classes. Consider a large function with many variables
[L80] declared within it. Let’s say you want to extract one small part of that
[L81] function into a separate function. However, the code you want to extract
[L82] uses four of the variables declared in the function. Must you pass all four of
[L83] those variables into the new function as arguments?
