[L9] G21: Understand the Algorithm
[L10] 
[L11] Lots of very funny code is written because people don’t take the time to
[L12] understand the algorithm. They get something to work by plugging in
[L13] enough if statements and flags, without really stopping to consider what is
[L14] really going on.
[L15] 
[L16] Programming is often an exploration. You think you know the right
[L17] algorithm for something, but then you wind up fiddling with it, prodding
[L18] and poking at it, until you get it to “work.” How do you know it “works”?
[L19] Because it passes the test cases you can think of.
[L20] 
[L21] There is nothing wrong with this approach. Indeed, often it is the only way
[L22] to get a function to do what you think it should. However, it is not sufficient
[L23] to leave the quotation marks around the word “work.”
[L24] 
[L25] Before you consider yourself to be done with a function, make sure you
[L26] understand how it works. It is not good enough that it passes all the tests.
[L27] You must know10 that the solution is correct.
[L28] 10. There is a difference between knowing how the code works and knowing whether the algorithm
[L29] will do the job required of it. Being unsure that an algorithm is appropriate is often a fact of life.
[L30] Being unsure what your code does is just laziness.
[L31] 
[L32] Often the best way to gain this knowledge and understanding is to refactor
[L33] the function into something that is so clean and expressive that it is obvious
[L34] how it works.
[L35] 
[L36] G22: Make Logical Dependencies Physical
[L37] 
[L38] If one module depends upon another, that dependency should be physical,
[L39] not just logical. The dependent module should not make assumptions (in
[L40] other words, logical dependencies) about the module it depends upon.
[L41] Rather it should explicitly ask that module for all the information it depends
[L42] upon.
[L43] 
[L44] For example, imagine that you are writing a function that prints a plain text
[L45] report of hours worked by employees. One class named HourlyReporter gathers
[L46] 
[L47] 
[L48] 
