[L1] The first thing you might notice is that the program got a lot longer. It went
[L2] from a little over one page to nearly three pages in length. There are several
[L3] reasons for this growth. First, the refactored program uses longer, more
[L4] descriptive variable names. Second, the refactored program uses function
[L5] and class declarations as a way to add commentary to the code. Third, we
[L6] used whitespace and formatting techniques to keep the program readable.
[L7] 
[L8] Notice how the program has been split into three main responsibilities. The
[L9] main program is contained in the PrimePrinter class all by itself. Its
[L10] responsibility is to handle the execution environment. It will change if the
[L11] method of invocation changes. For example, if this program were converted
[L12] to a SOAP service, this is the class that would be affected.
[L13] 
[L14] The RowColumnPagePrinter knows all about how to format a list of numbers into
[L15] pages with a certain number of rows and columns. If the formatting of the
[L16] output needed changing, then this is the class that would be affected.
[L17] 
[L18] 
[L19] 
[L20] The PrimeGenerator class knows how to generate a list prime numbers. Notice
[L21] that it is not meant to be instantiated as an object. The class is just a useful
[L22] scope in which its variables can be declared and kept hidden. This class will
[L23] change if the algorithm for computing prime numbers changes.
[L24] 
[L25] This was not a rewrite! We did not start over from scratch and write the
[L26] program over again. Indeed, if you look closely at the two different
[L27] programs, you’ll see that they use the same algorithm and mechanics to get
[L28] their work done.
[L29] 
[L30] The change was made by writing a test suite that verified the precise
[L31] behavior of the first program. Then a myriad of tiny little changes were
[L32] made, one at a time. After each change the program was executed to ensure
[L33] that the behavior had not changed. One tiny step after another, the first
[L34] program was cleaned up and transformed into the second.
[L35] 
[L36] 
[L37] 
[L38] Organizing for Change
[L39] 
[L40] For most systems, change is continual. Every change subjects us to the risk
[L41] that the remainder of the system no longer works as intended. In a clean
[L42] system we organize our classes so as to reduce the risk of change.
[L43] 
[L44] The Sql class in Listing 10-9 is used to generate properly formed SQL strings
[L45] given appropriate metadata. It’s a work in progress and, as such, doesn’t yet
[L46] support SQL functionality like update statements. When the time comes for
[L47] the Sql class to support an update statement, we’ll have to “open up” this class
[L48] to make modifications. The problem with opening a class is that it
[L49] introduces risk. Any modifications to the class have the potential of
[L50] breaking other code in the class. It must be fully retested.
[L51] 
[L52] Listing 10-9 A class that must be opened for change
[L53]    public class Sql {   public Sql(String table, Column[] columns)
[L54]       public String create()
[L55]       public String insert(Object[] fields)
[L56]       public String selectAll()
[L57]       public String findByKey(String keyColumn, String keyValue)
[L58]       public String select(Column column, String pattern)
[L59]       public String select(Criteria criteria)
[L60]       public String preparedInsert()
[L61]       private String columnList(Column[] columns)
[L62]       private String valuesList(Object[] fields, final Column[] columns)
[L63]       private String selectWithCriteria(String criteria)
[L64]       private String placeholderList(Column[] columns)
[L65]    }
[L66] 
[L67] The Sql class must change when we add a new type of statement. It also
[L68] must change when we alter the details of a single statement type—for
[L69] example, if we need to modify the select functionality to support subselects.
[L70] These two reasons to change mean that the Sql class violates the SRP.
