[L10] Concurrency Defense Principles
[L11] 
[L12] What follows is a series of principles and techniques for defending your
[L13] systems from the problems of concurrent code.
[L14] 
[L15] Single Responsibility Principle
[L16] 
[L17] The SRP5 states that a given method/class/component should have a single
[L18] reason to change. Concurrency design is complex enough to be a reason to
[L19] change in it’s own right and therefore deserves to be separated from the rest
[L20] of the code. Unfortunately, it is all too common for concurrency
[L21] implementation details to be embedded directly into other production code.
[L22] Here are a few things to consider:
[L23] 5. [PPP]
[L24] 
[L25] • Concurrency-related code has its own life cycle of development,
[L26] change, and tuning.
[L27] 
[L28] • Concurrency-related code has its own challenges, which are different
[L29] from and often more difficult than nonconcurrency-related code.
[L30] 
[L31] • The number of ways in which miswritten concurrency-based code can
[L32] fail makes it challenging enough without the added burden of
[L33] surrounding application code.
[L34] 
[L35] Recommendation: Keep your concurrency-related code separate from
[L36] other code.6
[L37] 
[L38] 6. See “Client/Server Example” on page 317.
[L39] 
[L40] Corollary: Limit the Scope of Data
[L41] 
[L42] As we saw, two threads modifying the same field of a shared object can
[L43] interfere with each other, causing unexpected behavior. One solution is to
[L44] use the synchronized keyword to protect a critical section in the code that uses
[L45] the shared object. It is important to restrict the number of such critical
[L46] sections. The more places shared data can get updated, the more likely:
[L47] 
[L48] • You will forget to protect one or more of those places—effectively
[L49] breaking all code that modifies that shared data.
[L50] 
[L51] 
[L52] 
[L53] • There will be duplication of effort required to make sure everything is
[L54] effectively guarded (violation of DRY7).
[L55] 
[L56] 7. [PRAG].
[L57] 
[L58] • It will be difficult to determine the source of failures, which are already
[L59] hard enough to find.
[L60] 
[L61] Recommendation: Take data encapsulation to heart; severely limit the
[L62] access of any data that may be shared.
[L63] 
[L64] Corollary: Use Copies of Data
[L65] 
[L66] A good way to avoid shared data is to avoid sharing the data in the first
[L67] place. In some situations it is possible to copy objects and treat them as
[L68] read-only. In other cases it might be possible to copy objects, collect results
[L69] from multiple threads in these copies and then merge the results in a single
[L70] thread.
[L71] 
[L72] If there is an easy way to avoid sharing objects, the resulting code will be
[L73] far less likely to cause problems. You might be concerned about the cost of
[L74] all the extra object creation. It is worth experimenting to find out if this is in
[L75] fact a problem. However, if using copies of objects allows the code to avoid
[L76] synchronizing, the savings in avoiding the intrinsic lock will likely make up
[L77] for the additional creation and garbage collection overhead.
[L78] 
[L79] Corollary: Threads Should Be as Independent as Possible
[L80] 
[L81] Consider writing your threaded code such that each thread exists in its own
[L82] world, sharing no data with any other thread. Each thread processes one
[L83] client request, with all of its required data coming from an unshared source
[L84] and stored as local variables. This makes each of those threads behave as if
[L85] it were the only thread in the world and there were no synchronization
[L86] requirements.
