[L41] Class Organization
[L42] 
[L43] Following the standard Java convention, a class should begin with a list of
[L44] variables. Public static constants, if any, should come first. Then private
[L45] static variables, followed by private instance variables. There is seldom a
[L46] good reason to have a public variable.
[L47] 
[L48] Public functions should follow the list of variables. We like to put the
[L49] private utilities called by a public function right after the public function
[L50] itself. This follows the stepdown rule and helps the program read like a
[L51] newspaper article.
[L52] 
[L53] Encapsulation
[L54] 
[L55] We like to keep our variables and utility functions private, but we’re not
[L56] fanatic about it. Sometimes we need to make a variable or utility function
[L57] protected so that it can be accessed by a test. For us, tests rule. If a test in
[L58] the same package needs to call a function or access a variable, we’ll make it
[L59] protected or package scope. However, we’ll first look for a way to maintain
[L60] privacy. Loosening encapsulation is always a last resort.
[L61] 
[L62] 
[L63] 
[L64] Classes Should Be Small!
[L65] 
[L66] The first rule of classes is that they should be small. The second rule of
[L67] classes is that they should be smaller than that. No, we’re not going to
[L68] repeat the exact same text from the Functions chapter. But as with
[L69] functions, smaller is the primary rule when it comes to designing classes.
[L70] As with functions, our immediate question is always “How small?”
