[L1] Let’s look at java.util.Map as an example. As you can see by examining Figure
[L2] 8-1, Maps have a very broad interface with plenty of capabilities. Certainly
[L3] this power and flexibility is useful, but it can also be a liability. For
[L4] instance, our application might build up a Map and pass it around. Our
[L5] intention might be that none of the recipients of our Map delete anything in
[L6] the map. But right there at the top of the list is the clear() method. Any user
[L7] of the Map has the power to clear it. Or maybe our design convention is that
[L8] only particular types of objects can be stored in the Map, but Maps do not
[L9] reliably constrain the types of objects placed within them. Any determined
[L10] user can add items of any type to any Map.
[L11] 
[L12] Figure 8-1 The methods of Map
[L13] 
[L14] 
[L15] 
[L16] If our application needs a Map of Sensors, you might find the sensors set up like
[L17] this:
[L18]    Map sensors = new HashMap();
[L19] 
[L20] Then, when some other part of the code needs to access the sensor, you see
[L21] this code:
[L22]    Sensor s = (Sensor)sensors.get(sensorId );
[L23] 
[L24] We don’t just see it once, but over and over again throughout the code. The
[L25] client of this code carries the responsibility of getting an Object from the Map
[L26] and casting it to the right type. This works, but it’s not clean code. Also, this
[L27] code does not tell its story as well as it could. The readability of this code
[L28] can be greatly improved by using generics, as shown below:
[L29]        Map<Sensor> sensors = new HashMap<Sensor>();
[L30]    …
[L31]        Sensor s = sensors.get(sensorId );
[L32] 
[L33] However, this doesn’t solve the problem that Map<Sensor> provides more
[L34] capability than we need or want.
[L35] 
[L36] Passing an instance of Map<Sensor> liberally around the system means that there
[L37] will be a lot of places to fix if the interface to Map ever changes. You might
[L38] 
[L39] 
[L40] 
[L41] think such a change to be unlikely, but remember that it changed when
[L42] generics support was added in Java 5. Indeed, we’ve seen systems that are
[L43] inhibited from using generics because of the sheer magnitude of changes
[L44] needed to make up for the liberal use of Maps.
